        "title": "Watchmaking - 04 The Marine Chronometer",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Harrison was a carpenter's son from Foulby, Yorkshire. Self-taught, he learned clockmaking from technical books and from making his own tools. His first notable work was a wooden movement turret clock (c. 1713) for Brocklesby Park in Lincolnshire — a remarkable piece that used lignum vitae (an extremely hard, naturally oily wood) for bearing surfaces, eliminating the need for lubrication. It ran for over 300 years with minimal maintenance."
    },
    {
      "id": "04-MARINE-CHRONOMETER.md#020",
      "path": [
        "04-MARINE-CHRONOMETER.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
